faq:
  - q: "How do you write a resume for a career change?"
    a: "Lead with a strong professional summary that bridges your background to your target role. Identify and lead with transferable skills. Reframe your experience descriptions to use the language of your target field. Add a prominent Skills section targeting the keywords of your new field. If you have done any projects, freelance work, or coursework in the new direction, include them."
  - q: "What transferable skills matter most for career changers?"
    a: "Depends on the target. Software engineers moving to PM: systems thinking, user-centric product decisions, cross-functional execution. Finance professionals moving to business operations: financial modeling, data-driven decision-making, stakeholder communication. Consultants moving to strategy/ops: problem structuring, stakeholder management, data-driven analysis. The key is translating the skill into the language of the target field — not listing it as you would have in your old field."
  - q: "Should I explain my career change in my resume?"
    a: "Briefly — in the professional summary. One to two sentences: what you are moving from, what you are moving toward, and the connecting logic. Do not over-explain or apologize. The resume shows capability; the cover letter or interview handles the narrative in more depth."
  - q: "How do you handle a career change with ATS?"
    a: "ATS optimizes for keyword match between your resume and the job description. Career changers often lack the exact keywords of their target field. Fix: identify the 10–15 most important keywords from target job descriptions and integrate them explicitly — in your Skills section, in your summary, and where accurate in your experience."
  - q: "Is it harder to get interviews when changing careers?"
    a: "Yes — but the gap is usually smaller than it feels. The biggest barrier is not qualification — it is unclear positioning. When your resume reads as a confused hybrid of your old field and new direction, recruiters skip it. When it reads as a clear candidate for the new role with relevant transferable strengths, it converts."
